<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zeno of Citium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 334–c. 262 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zeno of Citium was a philosopher from Kition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stoicism">Stoicism</a>. Arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> around 312 BCE, he studied with the Cynic Crates and others, and from about 300 BCE taught in the Stoa Poikile, the painted colonnade that gave the school its name. He taught that virtue, living in agreement with nature and reason, is the only good, ideas developed by his successors Cleanthes and Chrysippus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zeno-of-citium.jpg" alt="Zeno of Citium" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Monti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paolo_Monti_-_Servizio_fotografico_(Napoli,_1969)_-_BEIC_6353768.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
